 - Bash: wc -l lib/index.js; echo "--- blame raw"; git blame -w -M -C --line-porcelain lib/index.js | grep '^author ' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -15; echo "--- blame ignoring reformat/annotate"; git blame -w -M -C --ignore-rev 62628c0 --line-porcelain lib/index.js | grep '^author ' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -15; echo "--- recent 24mo"; git shortlog -sn --since='24 months ago' HEAD -- lib; echo "--- last commit dates"; git log -3 --format='%ad %an %s' --date=short -- lib
FINAL ANSWER:
**Bus factor is 1.** Troy Goode alone covers most of `lib`.

- **Scope:** `lib` has one file, `lib/index.js`, with 238 lines and 25 authors in its history.
- **Surviving lines:** I counted with `git blame -w -M -C` and skipped the `62628c0` reformat commit. Troy Goode owns 159 of 238 lines, or 67%. That is past 50% on one person. The next authors are Douglas Wilson (22), Alejandro Caravaca Puchades (12, plus 4 under the alias `alex94puchades`) and Raymond Feng (7). Everyone else has 6 lines or fewer.
- **Duplicate identity:** `troy@laptop.example` is also Troy Goode. It adds to the 159 lines in the "ownership" count but doesn't change the result.
- **Recent activity:** Over the last 24 months, only two authors touched `lib`: Troy Goode (`lib: annotate handlers`, 2026-04-10) and "Fiona Formatter". Fiona's single commit is a whitespace/quote reformat, so it adds no understanding of the code. Before the 2026-04-10 commit, the last change was from LuisEGR in 2021-07 (a lint cleanup).

**Answer to your question:** if Troy Goode leaves, nobody who remains owns more than about 9% of the lines. That includes Douglas Wilson, the next author. Losing Troy removes the only active owner. The remaining authors are historical contributors, most of whom committed once.

Raw blame is misleading here. Without `--ignore-rev`, "Fiona Formatter" shows 32 lines of ownership from the reformat. That is formatting, not knowledge.

The file is small (238 lines), so the cost of someone new learning it is low, even though ownership is concentrated.

